// The fixture that decides the increment.
//
// `net_roundtrip.nova` proves the bytes come back correct, but a fully
// blocking `connect`/`read`/`write` underneath the identical Nova-level
// surface would produce the identical correct bytes there too -- an
// end-to-end test that only checks the final result cannot tell a real
// poller from a busy spin or an outright blocking call, because all three
// eventually produce the same answer. This fixture is the one that can: it
// spawns a counter alongside a socket read and asserts that the counter's
// own output lands **between** the socket task's "wrote" and "read" lines,
// not merely somewhere in the whole program's output.
//
// **`connect` runs in `main`, before either task is spawned -- deliberately.**
// `crates/nova-runtime/src/net.rs`'s `connect` always parks exactly once on
// its first poll no matter how fast the underlying syscall actually settles
// (`start_connect` folds "genuinely would-block" and "already succeeded"
// into the identical `Started::WouldBlock` outcome -- see that module's own
// doc comment). A cooperative executor drains its whole ready queue before
// ever blocking on I/O, so if `connect` were called *inside* the spawned
// reader task, `counter` would run to completion during *that* park --
// before the socket task ever reaches its write or its read -- and have
// nothing left to interleave with during the read's own wait. Connecting in
// `main` first removes that confound: the only suspension inside the
// interleaving window this fixture measures is the read.
//
// The paired server (`crates/nova-cli/tests/run_tests.rs`'s `EchoServer`,
// `net_interleave_run`) sleeps briefly after accepting, before echoing
// anything back, so the reader's `read` is guaranteed to find nothing yet
// and genuinely suspend rather than racing real loopback latency.
//
// The port file this reads is named for this fixture alone, not this
// process -- like every other `std/net` fixture's own port file (see
// `net_roundtrip.nova`'s header for the accepted hazard that follows from
// that).
//
// **Added 2026-08-16 (final whole-branch review): exactly what this fixture
// guards, and what it does not.** The design spec
// (`docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-15-io-poller-and-std-net-design.md` §5)
// named a blocking `connect` as the mutation this fixture exists to kill.
// It does not kill that one. Moving `connect` into `main` -- correct for the
// confound described above -- also moves it out of the window this fixture
// measures, so a `connect` made genuinely OS-blocking on its own passes here
// and everywhere else in the suite.
//
// What this fixture does kill is **the read-blocking shape**: a `read` that
// blocks the executor's thread instead of parking, whether directly or as a
// consequence of the socket never being set non-blocking at all. That is the
// property the interleaving assertion actually measures, and it is the one
// worth having -- the read is where a real program waits.
//
// An isolated connect-only blocking syscall is **not guarded**, here or
// anywhere. No structural guard was built for it deliberately:
// `net.rs`'s `start_connect` folds "would block" and "already succeeded" into
// the identical `Started::WouldBlock` (see above), and un-folding them to
// assert on the difference is platform-fragile, because a loopback `connect`
// legitimately returns `rc == 0` on Linux. That would trade a documented gap
// for a flaky test. The gap is recorded in the design spec's §7 as well, dated
// the same day, rather than left implied by this fixture's name.
async fn count_to_three() -> Int {
    let mut i = 0
    while i < 3 {
        println("counter: ${i}")
        yield_now().await
        i = i + 1
    }
    i
}

async fn read_after_connecting(s: TcpStream) -> Int {
    let n = match s.write(bytes_from_string("ping")).await {
        Ok(count) => count
        Err(e) => panic("write: ${e.message}")
    }
    println("reader: wrote ${n}")
    let data = match s.read(64).await {
        Ok(b) => b
        Err(e) => panic("read: ${e.message}")
    }
    match data.to_string() {
        Some(text) => println("reader: read ${text}")
        None => println("reader: read <non-utf8>")
    }
    let _ = s.close().await
    n
}

async fn main() {
    let port = match read_to_string("${temp_dir()}/nova_net_interleave_port.txt").await {
        Ok(p) => p.trim()
        Err(e) => panic("read port file: ${e.message}")
    }
    let addr = "127.0.0.1:${port}"
    let s = match connect(addr).await {
        Ok(s) => s
        Err(e) => panic("connect: ${e.message}")
    }

    let reader = spawn(read_after_connecting(s))
    let counter = spawn(count_to_three())
    let _ = reader.join().await
    let _ = counter.join().await
}
